-- A pending first half is refused **where the next token is read**, not merely left
-- unfinished at the end of the literal: without that, the `\uDCA9` here would pair
-- across the `x` and the literal would be the two-character `x💩` — a value 18.6
-- refuses. The byte-escape and doubled-quote arms take the same guard, in
-- `a_first_surrogate_followed_by_a_byte_escape_is_refused` and
-- `a_half_does_not_survive_a_doubled_quote_in_an_e_string`.
-- @test
-- @expect-error: a plain character cannot sit inside a pair
-- @no-error-fields: pglet's `P` is the string literal's own start where 18.6's is the
-- offending escape inside it (and, for an unpaired first half, the token the `xeu`
-- state fails at). That granularity is the lexer's everywhere, not this rule's.
SELECT E'\uD83Dx\uDCA9';
